    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I'll provide the technical details, but I'll still try to incorporate a brief, non-technical mention of goblins to keep the analogy going.\n\nLisinopril, an ACE inhibitor, works by blocking the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Here's a step-by-step explanation of the RAAS pathway:\n\n1. **Renin release**: Renin is released by the juxtaglomerular cells in the kidneys, triggered by decreased renal blood flow, increased sympathetic tone, or decreased sodium delivery to the distal tubule. This is a critical step in the RAAS pathway.\n\n2. **Angiotensinogen conversion**: Renin converts angiotensinogen to angiotensin I. This is a crucial step in the RAAS pathway, as angiotensin I is then converted to angiotensin II.\n\n3. **Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) activation**: Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) is a transmembrane enzyme that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Angiotensin II is a potent vasoconstrictor and stimulates the release of aldosterone from the adrenal glands.\n\n4",
      "source": "target",
